RENAME(1)                     Funny CLI Manual                     RENAME(1)

NAME
    rename — Rename a file or directory, because names can be changed.

SYNOPSIS
    rename <old> <new>

DESCRIPTION
    The rename command changes the name or path of a file or directory.

    The contents of the file or directory are not changed. Only its
    name or location is modified.

USAGE
    > rename <old> <new>       # Renames the specified file or directory.

FUN FACTS
    - rename changes the name without changing what is inside.
    - A file can go from boring.txt to amazing.txt without changing a
    single byte.
    - You can also use rename to move something to another path.
    - Giving something a new name does not make it a new thing.

EXAMPLES
    > rename old.txt new.txt
        Renames old.txt to new.txt.

    > rename draft.txt final.txt
        Renames draft.txt to final.txt.

    > rename documents projects
        Renames the documents directory to projects.

    > rename notes.txt documents/notes.txt
        Renames notes.txt and moves it into the documents directory.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), ls(1), mv(1), cp(1), rm(1), mkdir(1), man(1)

    Remember, rename does not change what's inside.
    It just gives it a new identity.

